    "body": "Tesla had 103 job openings with Semi in the title posted at its Sparks, Nevada, truck plant as of Oct. 16, according to Tesla’s careers site . The listings come three weeks after the plant started high-volume Semi production on Sept. 24.\nThe hiring is for a 1.7-million-square-foot factory designed to build 50,000 trucks a year, as fleets line up orders for a truck Tesla first showed in 2017. Tesla has not said how many Semis the plant is building now. Before the dedicated line opened, the Semi fleet numbered just under 200 pilot trucks, delivered to customers including DHL, PepsiCo and US Foods.\nDrayage carrier IMC Logistics ordere d 50 Semis this month, following WattEV’s 370-t r uck order in May. Tesla was also named lead manufacturer on a 2,500-truck order through the ZET SCALE alliance in September, though the alliance did not say how many of those trucks will be Semis.\nTesla CEO Elon Musk told analysts in July, “We have started production with the Tesla semi-truck.” The company’s second-quarter shareholder update said production was expected to begin later in the year .\nSemi openings span assembly to dispatch\nThe Semi listings include quality technicians for stamping, body-in-white and powertrain; equipment maintenance technicians for general assembly and the drivetrain; and a CDL driver, a dispatcher and an endurance fleet driver.\nSeveral are night-shift roles, including a battery production supervisor, associate production managers and stamping quality technicians. Tesla is also hiring construction staff for the Semi site, from a pipefitter to an electrical superintendent.\nThe Semi roles are part of a larger list: Tesla’s site showed 490 openings in Sparks, 301 of them in manufacturing. The company’s Sparks listings on Indeed include production roles on battery, Powerwall, drive unit and LFP cell lines.\nSemi service and charging build out beyond Nevada\nAnother 66 Semi postings sit outside Sparks. In the U.S., Tesla is hiring Semi service managers, supervisors and technical instructors in 11 cities, including Laredo, Texas; Elizabeth, New Jersey; and Chicago.\nOn the charging side, Pilot and Tesla opened their first Megacharger site in Ellabell, Georgia, Oct. 8, with 12 more under construction.\nTesla is also hiring for the Semi in Germany, France, the Netherlands, Belgium and the U.K., where it is targeting customer deliveries by the end of 2027 . Postings there include service technicians in Berlin, Düsseldorf and Lyon, France.\nThe first European trucks will come from Nevada. “We’ve got ample capacity to support our initial wave,” Dan Priestley, who leads the Semi program, said in a September keynote at IAA Transportation in Hannover, Germany.\nOutput figures remain undisclosed\nTesla’s third-quarter delivery report on Oct. 2 did not mention the Semi, and the company has not disclosed pricing. It reports quarterly results after the market closes Oct. 21.\nSemi postings are down from the spring even as Tesla’s overall Nevada postings rose. Stocktwits counted 146 Semi roles among nearly 471 full-time Nevada jobs on Tesla’s site in April, though it did not say how it identified Semi roles. On Oct. 16, the site listed 103 Semi-titled roles and 537 full-time Nevada openings.\nPriestley has also said “Tesla is not a heavy truck company yet,” FreightWaves reported in its coverage of the Sept. 24 inauguration.\nWhy it matters: Tesla is staffing a Nevada plant built for 50,000 trucks a year as IMC, WattEV and ZET SCALE orders arrive. It has not said how many Semis the plant builds today, and its Oct. 21 results are the next chance to find out.\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
